ARM: add ARCH_RP2350 with a TIMER0 clocksource and a core-1 ring console

Machine support for the Arm side of the RP2350, plus the two drivers the board
cannot boot without. Both drivers live in the machine directory rather than
under drivers/, so the port touches exactly two existing files.

ARCH_RP2350 selects ARM_NO_EXCLUSIVES rather than offering it. LDREX/STREX on
this SoC work only in the internal SRAM and never succeed in the PSRAM that
holds system RAM, and they fail silently rather than faulting -- a kernel built
without it does not crash, it livelocks in the first cmpxchg with no diagnostic.
That is not a choice to leave to whoever runs menuconfig.

timer-rp2350 drives TIMER0, a 64-bit 1 MHz counter with four alarms, as both
clocksource and clockevent. ARMV7M_SYSTICK, which the other v7-M platforms use,
registers only a clocksource. Alarm 0 is the tick; the other three are left for
firmware on core 1. set_next_event checks ARMED and re-reads the counter,
because an alarm programmed for a time that has already passed disarms without
firing and would silently stop the tick rather than fail.

frank-ring is the kernel end of the console. The RP2350's USB controller has no
Linux driver, so core 1 runs a TinyUSB CDC device and moves bytes through two
rings in SRAM; this driver is a polled tty that knows nothing about USB. There
is no locking because each ring has one producer and one consumer on fixed,
different cores -- necessary rather than tidy, since with ARM_NO_EXCLUSIVES the
kernel's atomics are interrupt-masked and protect nothing against core 1. Only
the ordering matters, and it is explicit. Console writes are bounded rather than
blocking: core 1 stops draining when no USB host is reading, and wedging the
machine because nobody is watching would be worse than dropping output.

